# Go Backend Rules

Applies to everything under `server/`.

- Structure: `internal/<domain>` per business area (products, inventory, pos, rma,
  procurement, accounting, auth) as set up in spec/00-overview.md. Each domain package
  exposes a service layer (plain functions or a struct with methods) that `cmd/api`
  wires into HTTP handlers. Handlers do not contain business logic.
- Database access: use a single `db` package for the connection pool and transaction
  helpers. Every multi-step write (e.g., "mark serial sold + create invoice_item + check
  stock") goes through an explicit `WithTransaction(ctx, func(tx) error {...})` wrapper —
  no bare multi-statement writes outside a transaction.
- Errors: define domain-specific sentinel errors (e.g., `ErrSerialNotAvailable`,
  `ErrInsufficientStock`) in each domain package. Handlers map these to HTTP status codes
  in one place (a shared error-to-status mapper), not ad hoc per handler.
- Concurrency: any stock/serial mutation must use `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` (or equivalent
  row locking) inside the transaction to prevent two concurrent requests from selling the
  same serial or overselling bulk stock. This is required, not optional — see
  spec/02-build-order.md Step 2 for the specific race condition to guard against.
- Config: all configuration (DB connection string, port, JWT secret, VAT defaults if
  any) loaded once at startup via a single `config` package reading environment
  variables, with explicit required/optional fields and sane validation on startup
  (fail fast if a required var is missing — don't fail silently later at runtime).
- Testing: table-driven tests (`[]struct{ name string; ... }` + `t.Run`) for service-layer
  logic. Use a real test Postgres instance (via `docker-compose` or testcontainers) for
  integration tests that hit the database — do not mock the database for logic that
  depends on transactional/locking behavior, since that's exactly what needs verifying.
- Migrations: one migration file per logical change, never edit a migration that has
  already been applied in any shared environment — add a new one instead.
- No global mutable state. Dependencies (DB pool, config, logger) are passed explicitly
  (constructor injection), not package-level singletons, so tests can substitute them.
